00:08:23.780 --> 00:08:26.030
They have a lot of
legacy applications.

00:08:26.030 --> 00:08:28.702
And they now want to
move to the cloud, right?

00:08:28.702 --> 00:08:30.410
They don't want to
refactor, because they

00:08:30.410 --> 00:08:32.243
have thousands of
thousands of applications,

00:08:32.243 --> 00:08:35.450
so they want to move as is with
minimal refactoring in a very

00:08:35.450 --> 00:08:37.440
short time period.

00:08:37.440 --> 00:08:40.280
So the question is, for
customers with such Windows

00:08:40.280 --> 00:08:42.380
and SQL Server workloads,
what support do we

00:08:42.380 --> 00:08:44.990
have in the platform for them?

00:08:44.990 --> 00:08:48.082
So let's look at a
little bit of that.

00:08:48.082 --> 00:08:50.040
One question I get asked
a lot, and therefore I

00:08:50.040 --> 00:08:53.120
like to address this up front,
is what flavors of Windows

00:08:53.120 --> 00:08:54.010
do you support?

00:08:54.010 --> 00:08:56.420
Do you support the
latest flavors?

00:08:56.420 --> 00:08:58.850
But we do understand
that some customers

00:08:58.850 --> 00:09:01.850
need to have their own time
frame to how they upgrade.

00:09:01.850 --> 00:09:04.790
So we also have
support for legacy OSs.

00:09:04.790 --> 00:09:07.130
So with the latest and
the greatest, which

00:09:07.130 --> 00:09:10.250
is 1709 and 1803
images, we also have

00:09:10.250 --> 00:09:12.720
via a migration tooling
support for legacy

00:09:12.720 --> 00:09:19.010
OSs like 2003 and 2008R1.

00:09:19.010 --> 00:09:23.630
When customers want to migrate,
they like to know, OK, great,

00:09:23.630 --> 00:09:25.880
the platform supports
this version.

00:09:25.880 --> 00:09:27.830
How do I actually migrate?

00:09:27.830 --> 00:09:29.300
And over the last
few months, we've

00:09:29.300 --> 00:09:31.910
been working a lot on
our migration story,

00:09:31.910 --> 00:09:35.570
giving you different options
based on your scenarios.

00:09:35.570 --> 00:09:38.450
There are three options that I
would like to go through today.

00:09:38.450 --> 00:09:40.220
One is the Virtual Disk Import.

00:09:40.220 --> 00:09:44.810
The second one is our VM
migration capabilities

00:09:44.810 --> 00:09:45.980
through Velostrata.

00:09:45.980 --> 00:09:47.890
And the third one
is CloudEndure.

00:09:47.890 --> 00:09:50.846
The first one is really good
if you have a few golden images

00:09:50.846 --> 00:09:52.220
that are [INAUDIBLE]
golden disks

00:09:52.220 --> 00:09:54.636
and that's the way you want
to spin up your infrastructure

00:09:54.636 --> 00:09:58.850
on premise and use the
same image to spin up

00:09:58.850 --> 00:10:01.670
your infrastructure
on the Cloud.

00:10:01.670 --> 00:10:04.610
We recently announced our
acquisition of Velostrata,

00:10:04.610 --> 00:10:08.540
and that adds a really
rich set of capabilities

00:10:08.540 --> 00:10:12.070
to orchestrate mass migrations.

00:10:12.070 --> 00:10:14.680
Last but not the least, we
have a great partnership

00:10:14.680 --> 00:10:17.530
with CloudEndure and have
helped move many customers

00:10:17.530 --> 00:10:23.540
over using their SaaS
based VM migration tooling.

00:10:23.540 --> 00:10:26.310
So let's dig into this
a little bit more.

00:10:26.310 --> 00:10:29.270
So what is the
Virtual Disk Import?

00:10:29.270 --> 00:10:33.230
A lot of customers have
what you call golden images

00:10:33.230 --> 00:10:34.490
or golden disks.

00:10:34.490 --> 00:10:36.680
Now, this is your
software, your scripts

00:10:36.680 --> 00:10:39.500
and everything pulled
into one that is certified

00:10:39.500 --> 00:10:43.070
by your IT or security admins.

00:10:43.070 --> 00:10:45.680
And this is what is your
[INAUDIBLE] for spinning up

00:10:45.680 --> 00:10:48.140
infrastructure on premise.

00:10:48.140 --> 00:10:50.840
And you probably want to
use that same on Cloud

00:10:50.840 --> 00:10:52.710
when you spin up
infrastructure there.

00:10:52.710 --> 00:10:54.980
So we have a really
simple command line tool

00:10:54.980 --> 00:10:57.620
which you can download, and
with a few simple commands

00:10:57.620 --> 00:11:00.590
you can actually
convert that disk

00:11:00.590 --> 00:11:03.110
into an image in Google Cloud.

00:11:03.110 --> 00:11:04.940
And just like you
spin up infrastructure

00:11:04.940 --> 00:11:07.790
using our images, you
can use this custom image

00:11:07.790 --> 00:11:09.910
to spin up infrastructure.

00:11:09.910 --> 00:11:11.720
The same way as
you can import, you

00:11:11.720 --> 00:11:16.750
can also export from GCE using
the same command line tool.

00:11:20.134 --> 00:11:22.300
Next I'd like to talk a
little bit about Velostrata.

00:11:22.300 --> 00:11:26.880
As I mentioned, Velostrata has
an enterprise grade migration

00:11:26.880 --> 00:11:29.150
tooling, and we're
very, very happy

00:11:29.150 --> 00:11:32.240
that they are now part of Google
and all these capabilities are

00:11:32.240 --> 00:11:36.390
now available to our
customers within the platform.

00:11:36.390 --> 00:11:39.660
What is unique about
the Velostrata offering

00:11:39.660 --> 00:11:43.210
is they have a unique
streaming technology.

00:11:43.210 --> 00:11:44.850
Now, what this
does is it streams

00:11:44.850 --> 00:11:48.190
the virtual machine on demand.

00:11:48.190 --> 00:11:51.960
And you can actually have
that VM booted in Google Cloud

00:11:51.960 --> 00:11:54.670
without having all
the disks moved over.

00:11:54.670 --> 00:11:56.940
In most migration
technologies, you actually

00:11:56.940 --> 00:11:58.950
have a full copy
of the entire VM

00:11:58.950 --> 00:12:01.080
state, all the disks
attached to it,

00:12:01.080 --> 00:12:02.640
and then you're able to boot it.

00:12:02.640 --> 00:12:05.520
What this really gives you
is the flexibility of having

00:12:05.520 --> 00:12:08.400
that VM boot up
in minutes, verify

00:12:08.400 --> 00:12:11.400
that your applications look
good, and then give a thumbs up

00:12:11.400 --> 00:12:14.760
and continue that migration
story, or thumbs down,

00:12:14.760 --> 00:12:16.500
reboot, start again.

00:12:16.500 --> 00:12:19.110
You can also do things
like rightsize your VMs,

00:12:19.110 --> 00:12:22.650
add different policies and
checks, and then kind of

00:12:22.650 --> 00:12:24.450
continue your migration journey.

00:12:24.450 --> 00:12:27.070
So it gives you a
lot of flexibility.

00:12:27.070 --> 00:12:29.580
The second thing to
note about Velostrata

00:12:29.580 --> 00:12:33.270
is it integrates really
well with VMware.

00:12:33.270 --> 00:12:35.876
So vSphere and vCenter APIs.

00:12:35.876 --> 00:12:37.500
They've done a great
job in making sure

00:12:37.500 --> 00:12:38.770
that that works really well.

00:12:38.770 --> 00:12:40.978
So if you have Windows Server
workloads or SQL Server

00:12:40.978 --> 00:12:43.170
workloads running
on VMware, this

00:12:43.170 --> 00:12:46.110
provides you a seamless
way to kind of move

00:12:46.110 --> 00:12:52.150
your workloads from your VMware
workloads on premise to GCP.

00:12:52.150 --> 00:12:55.040
We've had a lot of sessions
that probably talk about this,

00:12:55.040 --> 00:12:57.670
and I'm happy to go
into more details.

00:12:57.670 --> 00:12:59.510
Let's talk about
our third solution.

00:12:59.510 --> 00:13:02.710
So we've been working with
CloudEndure for over 18 months.

00:13:02.710 --> 00:13:05.740
They're a great partner and
have helped multiple enterprise

00:13:05.740 --> 00:13:06.940
customers move.

00:13:06.940 --> 00:13:09.750
This is a great solution
if you're working

00:13:09.750 --> 00:13:12.190
in a multi-cloud environment.

00:13:12.190 --> 00:13:14.440
So CloudEndure
solution is really

00:13:14.440 --> 00:13:18.970
good at moving from
any source to GCP.

00:13:18.970 --> 00:13:22.270
The way they migrate is through
block level replication.

00:13:22.270 --> 00:13:24.640
And they have support
for all flavors

00:13:24.640 --> 00:13:27.522
of Windows as well as Linux.

00:13:27.522 --> 00:13:28.980
So you have three
different options

00:13:28.980 --> 00:13:31.146
based on your requirements,
based on your workloads,

00:13:31.146 --> 00:13:33.120
based on your timeline.

00:13:33.120 --> 00:13:37.650
You can move and pick and choose
and move your workloads to GCP.

00:13:37.650 --> 00:13:41.960
We also understand that in
migrations, you probably

00:13:41.960 --> 00:13:44.570
need more support than just
information about tooling.

00:13:44.570 --> 00:13:45.950
So we have an
organization, which

00:13:45.950 --> 00:13:48.470
is a professional
services organization,

00:13:48.470 --> 00:13:51.350
and this organization has worked
with many of our enterprise

00:13:51.350 --> 00:13:55.850
customers to assess their
enterprise environment,

00:13:55.850 --> 00:13:58.490
understand the needs
of it, do a POC,

00:13:58.490 --> 00:14:01.130
and then figure out what is
the best strategy to migrate

00:14:01.130 --> 00:14:02.450
to Google Cloud.

00:14:02.450 --> 00:14:07.731
So if you go to cloud.google.com
/solutions/migrationcenter,

00:14:07.731 --> 00:14:09.980
you'll have a lot more
information about the different

00:14:09.980 --> 00:14:11.362
plans available.

00:14:14.530 --> 00:14:16.900
Great, so we talked a little
bit about Windows Servers,

00:14:16.900 --> 00:14:18.742
we talked about the
migration tooling.

00:14:18.742 --> 00:14:20.200
Now let's talk
about your back end.

00:14:20.200 --> 00:14:23.830
So a lot of the Windows .NET
applications that we have have

00:14:23.830 --> 00:14:25.420
SQL Server as the back end.

00:14:25.420 --> 00:14:28.330
So customers ask me, what
are the images you support?

00:14:28.330 --> 00:14:31.420
Just like Windows, we try
to support all flavors

00:14:31.420 --> 00:14:33.580
and versions of SQL Server.

00:14:33.580 --> 00:14:36.610
And you also have to support
for two ways to license it.

00:14:36.610 --> 00:14:38.380
You can use our
images and you can

00:14:38.380 --> 00:14:43.540
pay as you use or you could
actually have your SQL Server

00:14:43.540 --> 00:14:47.080
licenses brought in if you
have software assurance,

00:14:47.080 --> 00:14:50.050
and that gives you an ability
to bring over your licenses

00:14:50.050 --> 00:14:51.621
through license mobility.

00:14:55.310 --> 00:14:57.300
When we think about
SQL server migrations,

00:14:57.300 --> 00:14:59.130
customers have
different thoughts.

00:14:59.130 --> 00:15:02.090
Some of our customers
use the migration tooling

00:15:02.090 --> 00:15:05.330
that we just spoke about,
and some of our customers

00:15:05.330 --> 00:15:07.590
have different
approaches to do that.

00:15:07.590 --> 00:15:09.480
So I'd like to talk
about three approaches.

00:15:09.480 --> 00:15:12.080
So the first one is a lot
of customers just spin up

00:15:12.080 --> 00:15:14.690
SQL Server VMs on
GCE and then they

00:15:14.690 --> 00:15:17.720
do a restore and backup of
their data from on premise

00:15:17.720 --> 00:15:20.367
onto Google Cloud.

00:15:20.367 --> 00:15:22.200
The second option is
we went through a bunch

00:15:22.200 --> 00:15:23.580
of our migration tooling.

00:15:23.580 --> 00:15:24.600
And if you have--

00:15:24.600 --> 00:15:27.740
specifically, if you are trying
to bring your own copy of SQL

00:15:27.740 --> 00:15:29.610
Server with the
license included,

00:15:29.610 --> 00:15:32.520
a lot of our customers use
some of the migration tooling

00:15:32.520 --> 00:15:34.900
that we just showcased.

00:15:34.900 --> 00:15:36.380
And the last one
is log shipping.

00:15:36.380 --> 00:15:38.890
So a lot of customers try
to do an availability group.

00:15:38.890 --> 00:15:43.270
They have a DR SQL
Server on Google Cloud.

00:15:43.270 --> 00:15:45.430
And they try to use
replication or log

00:15:45.430 --> 00:15:48.130
shipping, natively supported
technologies in SQL Server

00:15:48.130 --> 00:15:48.840
to cut over.

00:15:52.010 --> 00:15:53.840
So now that you've
actually cut over

00:15:53.840 --> 00:15:56.660
and landed your SQL
Server VM on GCE,

00:15:56.660 --> 00:15:59.091
you have your
application stack, how

00:15:59.091 --> 00:16:01.340
do you ensure that you get
some of the enterprise gate

00:16:01.340 --> 00:16:02.570
features?

00:16:02.570 --> 00:16:08.960
So we have support for the
two major recommendations

00:16:08.960 --> 00:16:11.770
from Microsoft for having
highly available SQL

00:16:11.770 --> 00:16:13.340
Servers on premise.

00:16:13.340 --> 00:16:15.260
So the first one is
availability groups.

00:16:15.260 --> 00:16:19.340
So you're probably familiar with
always on availability groups.

00:16:19.340 --> 00:16:21.980
And we have support for that.

00:16:21.980 --> 00:16:26.060
It gives you a regionally
highly available configuration.

00:16:26.060 --> 00:16:29.300
You can keep your SQL nodes
in two different zones

00:16:29.300 --> 00:16:32.780
in the same region or you
can have them cross region.

00:16:32.780 --> 00:16:34.310
The good thing
about this is it's

00:16:34.310 --> 00:16:36.620
a multi-site failable
cluster solution,

00:16:36.620 --> 00:16:39.000
and you really don't need
a load balancer for it.

00:16:39.000 --> 00:16:40.520
So if you look at
the documentation

00:16:40.520 --> 00:16:44.510
that we have with just
some simple networking

00:16:44.510 --> 00:16:47.000
rules and routing,
you can actually

00:16:47.000 --> 00:16:49.580
have the set up really quickly.

00:16:49.580 --> 00:16:52.680
We have a simple click
to deploy template.

00:16:52.680 --> 00:16:55.700
So if you go to our Cloud
Marketplace and search

00:16:55.700 --> 00:16:58.030
for SQL Server always
on availability groups,

00:16:58.030 --> 00:16:59.030
you will see a template.

00:16:59.030 --> 00:17:00.530
And with one click,
you can actually

00:17:00.530 --> 00:17:02.780
deploy this and test it out.

00:17:06.780 --> 00:17:09.119
The second option we see,
and this is typically

00:17:09.119 --> 00:17:13.619
for SQL Server VMs that
are running before 2012.

00:17:13.619 --> 00:17:16.290
So a lot of customers
have physical SANs.

00:17:16.290 --> 00:17:18.690
They have their databases
connected to that.

00:17:18.690 --> 00:17:21.510
A lot of customers
who have queries

00:17:21.510 --> 00:17:23.280
which are across
databases still want

00:17:23.280 --> 00:17:27.390
to have this configuration
for SQL Server HA.

00:17:27.390 --> 00:17:29.010
And the way to kind
of support that

00:17:29.010 --> 00:17:32.130
is we are now
figuring out how we

00:17:32.130 --> 00:17:35.310
can build a virtual
SAN on Google Cloud

00:17:35.310 --> 00:17:38.490
and then have both your
primary and secondary nodes

00:17:38.490 --> 00:17:39.760
connect to it.

00:17:39.760 --> 00:17:41.610
So the way we have
enabled this technology

00:17:41.610 --> 00:17:44.640
is by using Storage
Space Direct, which

00:17:44.640 --> 00:17:48.240
is a features supported
in Windows Server 2016.

00:17:48.240 --> 00:17:50.250
What this feature
allows you to do

00:17:50.250 --> 00:17:53.580
is it allows you to cluster
all the disks attached

00:17:53.580 --> 00:17:56.070
to the various nodes that you
have provisioned in a Windows

00:17:56.070 --> 00:17:59.580
cluster and present
that as a single volume

00:17:59.580 --> 00:18:03.152
to both the nodes on the system.

00:18:03.152 --> 00:18:04.110
So this is really cool.

00:18:04.110 --> 00:18:07.410
It actually gives you a
virtual SAN at a very low cost.

00:18:07.410 --> 00:18:11.040
It replicates your data
across these disks,

00:18:11.040 --> 00:18:14.590
giving you a pretty
highly reliable solution.

00:18:14.590 --> 00:18:15.930
So we have support for that.

00:18:15.930 --> 00:18:17.580
We have detailed documentations.

00:18:17.580 --> 00:18:19.590
And just like the
first use case,

00:18:19.590 --> 00:18:21.810
we have a one click deployment
template for that too.

00:18:26.440 --> 00:18:30.580
The third option is we've worked
with SIOS, who's a partner,

00:18:30.580 --> 00:18:32.650
and they have a solution
called Datakeeper.

00:18:32.650 --> 00:18:35.950
And this is very similar to
the SQL Server FCI solution

00:18:35.950 --> 00:18:39.040
where you can actually
have SQL Server clustering

00:18:39.040 --> 00:18:40.420
without the need for a SAN.

00:18:40.420 --> 00:18:44.830
So they also have a
virtual SAN technology.

00:18:44.830 --> 00:18:47.380
The difference
between SQL Server FCI

00:18:47.380 --> 00:18:50.230
that I showed a slide
before and this is you

00:18:50.230 --> 00:18:53.650
can actually use this
for versions of Windows

00:18:53.650 --> 00:18:54.610
before 2016.

00:18:54.610 --> 00:18:57.040
So if you have some
compatibility reasons where

00:18:57.040 --> 00:18:59.920
you want to have
Windows Server 2012

00:18:59.920 --> 00:19:03.350
and still want this
feature, this is an option.

00:19:03.350 --> 00:19:05.140
It's also a really
cost effective option

00:19:05.140 --> 00:19:08.030
because it works with
SQL Server Standard.

00:19:08.030 --> 00:19:09.490
So if you have SQL
Server Standard

00:19:09.490 --> 00:19:12.220
and you still want
to have HA, this

00:19:12.220 --> 00:19:14.890
is one way in which
you can support it.

00:19:19.590 --> 00:19:20.090
Cool.

00:19:20.090 --> 00:19:23.350
So just to recap, we talked
about how you can migrate stuff

00:19:23.350 --> 00:19:24.490
to Google Cloud.

00:19:24.490 --> 00:19:27.430
We talked a little bit about
SQL Server and the different HA

00:19:27.430 --> 00:19:31.820
technologies that
are supported on GCE.

00:19:31.820 --> 00:19:34.746
Last but not the least, people
want to know about identity.

00:19:34.746 --> 00:19:36.620
And there are three ways
in which we actually

00:19:36.620 --> 00:19:40.460
support different Active
Directory use cases.

00:19:40.460 --> 00:19:43.510
The first one is customers have
their users and groups defined

00:19:43.510 --> 00:19:46.420
on their on premise Active
Directory domain controller.

00:19:46.420 --> 00:19:48.850
And they just want to
sync this to Google Cloud

00:19:48.850 --> 00:19:51.670
so that they can assign
the right IAM permissions

00:19:51.670 --> 00:19:54.280
and give those users and groups
the right level of access

00:19:54.280 --> 00:19:55.690
on Google Cloud.

00:19:55.690 --> 00:19:57.550
And that is a
supported scenario.

00:19:57.550 --> 00:20:01.150
We have a sync tool that
actually syncs users and groups

00:20:01.150 --> 00:20:03.430
to a domain controller
on Google Cloud

00:20:03.430 --> 00:20:05.711
and allows you to
assign IAM permissions.

00:20:05.711 --> 00:20:07.960
This is great because you
actually don't have anything

00:20:07.960 --> 00:20:11.331
from Google Cloud reaching back
into your domain controller

00:20:11.331 --> 00:20:11.830
on premise.

00:20:11.830 --> 00:20:15.190
So it's a nice isolated feature.

00:20:15.190 --> 00:20:17.280
The two other ways to
move Active Directory

00:20:17.280 --> 00:20:21.780
is a hybrid approach or an
all in approach, as I call it.

00:20:21.780 --> 00:20:23.820
You can actually have a
full trust relationship

00:20:23.820 --> 00:20:26.370
between a domain
controller on premise

00:20:26.370 --> 00:20:29.580
and a domain controller
in Google Cloud.

00:20:29.580 --> 00:20:33.440
And you can achieve this
via a VPN or interconnect.

00:20:33.440 --> 00:20:36.410
The second option
is you actually

00:20:36.410 --> 00:20:40.190
move your domain controllers
to the different zones

00:20:40.190 --> 00:20:41.210
in a region.

00:20:41.210 --> 00:20:42.830
And this option
is actually better

00:20:42.830 --> 00:20:48.410
if you want a much higher
resilience and lower latencies,

00:20:48.410 --> 00:20:50.720
because the traffic is
all on Google's network

00:20:50.720 --> 00:20:52.550
and doesn't really
have to kind of go

00:20:52.550 --> 00:20:54.800
over VPN to your on premise.

00:20:54.800 --> 00:20:57.470
So different options
and different choices

00:20:57.470 --> 00:21:00.300
based on your needs
and scenarios.

00:21:00.300 --> 00:21:05.160
We cannot do justice to the
full thing of Active Directory,

00:21:05.160 --> 00:21:08.580
but there was a session at 3:15
and I hope most folks over here

00:21:08.580 --> 00:21:09.690
attended it.

00:21:09.690 --> 00:21:11.430
We had a customer Capital One.

00:21:11.430 --> 00:21:13.740
I see a few folks
who are nodding.

00:21:13.740 --> 00:21:17.040
So we had a deep dive in
each one of these methods.

00:21:17.040 --> 00:21:18.780
And we also had a
customer, Capital One,

00:21:18.780 --> 00:21:21.030
come and showcase how they
actually moved their Active

00:21:21.030 --> 00:21:22.200
Directory over.

00:21:22.200 --> 00:21:25.080
So if you missed that
session, please look up IO

00:21:25.080 --> 00:21:28.770
274 or come and
talk to us and we'll

00:21:28.770 --> 00:21:30.270
help you get more
information there.

00:21:33.881 --> 00:21:34.380
Cool.

00:21:34.380 --> 00:21:37.410
So last but not the least,
if you're a .NET developer

00:21:37.410 --> 00:21:40.350
on a platform, you
have a lot of support.

00:21:40.350 --> 00:21:42.750
So you have Cloud Tools
for Visual Studio.

00:21:42.750 --> 00:21:47.280
You also have support for
.NET Core, both on GKE,

00:21:47.280 --> 00:21:50.370
which is our Google Kubernetes
Engine, as well as GAE,

00:21:50.370 --> 00:21:52.240
which is Google App Engine.

00:21:52.240 --> 00:21:55.020
We've also added some really
cool features in Stackdriver,

00:21:55.020 --> 00:21:59.220
which allow you to
debug your .NET code,

00:21:59.220 --> 00:22:00.870
your .NET production code live.

00:22:00.870 --> 00:22:02.970
And Simon is going
to talk a little bit

00:22:02.970 --> 00:22:05.250
and show a little bit about
that in the next demo.

00:22:07.820 --> 00:22:09.680
So I'm going to hand
it over to Simon

00:22:09.680 --> 00:22:12.990
to demo how we use
some of these tools.

00:22:12.990 --> 00:22:15.600
So we're going to take a
really legacy application

00:22:15.600 --> 00:22:17.809
and just lift and
shift it to the Cloud.

00:22:17.809 --> 00:22:18.850
SIMON ZELTSER: All right.

00:22:18.850 --> 00:22:19.820
Thanks, Amruta.

00:22:19.820 --> 00:22:25.170
So let's talk about
this demo a little bit.

00:22:25.170 --> 00:22:27.650
So we are going to
talk about application,

00:22:27.650 --> 00:22:32.110
which is really old legacy
application from 2006,

00:22:32.110 --> 00:22:34.870
.NET Framework 2.0.

00:22:34.870 --> 00:22:37.520
There is a monolithic
web front end

00:22:37.520 --> 00:22:39.350
talking to multiple databases.

00:22:39.350 --> 00:22:43.640
And we are going to just lift
and shift this application

00:22:43.640 --> 00:22:45.330
as it is into Google Cloud.

00:22:48.430 --> 00:22:51.670
So let's go into the demo.

00:23:01.880 --> 00:23:03.710
OK.

00:23:03.710 --> 00:23:08.090
So let's first see what
our application does.

00:23:08.090 --> 00:23:13.070
So this is a really old
sample of e-commerce shop.

00:23:13.070 --> 00:23:16.760
With the old stack, you
can explore categories,

00:23:16.760 --> 00:23:19.400
you can buy stuff,
you can log in.

00:23:19.400 --> 00:23:23.730
So basically yet another
e-shop application.

00:23:23.730 --> 00:23:26.000
So let's go into
the Google Console

00:23:26.000 --> 00:23:31.050
and see that essentially
what we need to do

00:23:31.050 --> 00:23:34.350
is deploy it under
Compute Engine.

00:23:34.350 --> 00:23:36.370
So we will have two instances.

00:23:36.370 --> 00:23:38.910
One of them will run
IIS with the front end

00:23:38.910 --> 00:23:41.310
and another one
will run SQL Server

00:23:41.310 --> 00:23:43.080
with all those databases.

00:23:43.080 --> 00:23:48.180
Now, Amruta spoke about the
database piece quite a bit,

00:23:48.180 --> 00:23:51.300
so I'll focus on the IIS piece.

00:23:51.300 --> 00:23:54.190
So let's go into Visual
Studio for a minute.

00:23:54.190 --> 00:23:55.980
And this is the
code of our demo.

00:23:55.980 --> 00:23:59.590
Everything is available
and you can later on

00:23:59.590 --> 00:24:04.400
refer to the full example
that we are demoing here

00:24:04.400 --> 00:24:06.390
in the reference slides.

00:24:06.390 --> 00:24:10.350
So I downloaded Cloud
Tools for Visual Studio.

00:24:10.350 --> 00:24:14.040
And then if I go into the Tools
and then Google Cloud Tools

00:24:14.040 --> 00:24:17.930
and Show Cloud
Explorer, you can see

00:24:17.930 --> 00:24:20.130
different artifacts
of our Cloud,

00:24:20.130 --> 00:24:22.400
including Google Compute Engine.

00:24:22.400 --> 00:24:27.590
So if we want to deploy a new
instance of IIS up and running,

00:24:27.590 --> 00:24:32.360
I just do right click
and New ASP.NET instance.

00:24:32.360 --> 00:24:35.750
And that will open a
screen where I just

00:24:35.750 --> 00:24:39.260
need to hit on this button
and that will make it work.

00:24:39.260 --> 00:24:42.140
Basically, it will
one click deploy

00:24:42.140 --> 00:24:45.200
the VM, configure
Windows and IIS for me,

00:24:45.200 --> 00:24:48.890
as well as all the
networking settings

00:24:48.890 --> 00:24:50.810
that we need to set up.

00:24:50.810 --> 00:24:57.160
I can use this Cloud Tools
for some other stuff as well.

00:24:57.160 --> 00:24:59.580
It will make me a
lot more productive.

00:24:59.580 --> 00:25:02.130
For example, I can go
