00:25:23.880 --> 00:25:25.030
environment.

00:25:25.030 --> 00:25:32.311
So that's our first
demo, and over to Amruta.

00:25:32.311 --> 00:25:33.310
AMRUTA GULANIKAR: Great.

00:25:33.310 --> 00:25:35.130
So this was a really,
really simple application.

00:25:35.130 --> 00:25:36.570
And in a matter
of a few minutes,

00:25:36.570 --> 00:25:41.535
you could actually spin up both
the VM as well as the back end

00:25:41.535 --> 00:25:44.050
and have your application up
and running on Google Cloud.

00:25:46.890 --> 00:25:49.440
So let's kind of talk a little
bit more about this journey

00:25:49.440 --> 00:25:52.320
and how you can
modernize it more.

00:25:52.320 --> 00:25:54.940
So before we go into some
of the options available,

00:25:54.940 --> 00:25:57.900
the question is, why modernize?

00:25:57.900 --> 00:25:59.940
And talking to
customers, they come up

00:25:59.940 --> 00:26:03.122
with generally three
pieces of feedback.

00:26:03.122 --> 00:26:04.830
Folks want to modernize
because they want

00:26:04.830 --> 00:26:07.680
to reduce cost and complexity.

00:26:07.680 --> 00:26:10.227
If you just lift
and shift VMs as is,

00:26:10.227 --> 00:26:11.810
you're going to save
on hardware cost.

00:26:11.810 --> 00:26:14.250
You're going to run on
Google great infrastructure.

00:26:14.250 --> 00:26:17.340
But you still have a monolithic
application and you cannot size

00:26:17.340 --> 00:26:20.070
it or change it up easily.

00:26:20.070 --> 00:26:22.960
The second one is
speed and agility.

00:26:22.960 --> 00:26:26.022
So as you kind of saw,
the pet shop application,

00:26:26.022 --> 00:26:27.480
you kind of noticed
that there were

00:26:27.480 --> 00:26:29.040
a few different pieces to it.

00:26:29.040 --> 00:26:32.070
There was an ordering, there was
a catalog, there was a product,

00:26:32.070 --> 00:26:33.960
and there was a web front end.

00:26:33.960 --> 00:26:37.650
Now, let's say your site
becomes really, really popular

00:26:37.650 --> 00:26:39.277
and the load suddenly spikes.

00:26:39.277 --> 00:26:40.860
Let's say Black
Friday and people just

00:26:40.860 --> 00:26:43.080
want to buy a lot of goldfish.

00:26:43.080 --> 00:26:46.710
You don't have the ability to
very quickly scale up or scale

00:26:46.710 --> 00:26:50.500
down your ordering part
of that application.

00:26:50.500 --> 00:26:52.740
So people want to break
this up into micro services

00:26:52.740 --> 00:26:54.900
so they have the ability
to turn up and turn down

00:26:54.900 --> 00:26:57.770
things as the load changes.

00:26:57.770 --> 00:26:59.220
Third is cross-platform support.

00:26:59.220 --> 00:27:00.930
As we go into the
world of Cloud,

00:27:00.930 --> 00:27:02.910
customers want to
make sure that they

00:27:02.910 --> 00:27:06.620
have the flexibility of
running on multiple clouds

00:27:06.620 --> 00:27:08.400
and in multiple environments.

00:27:08.400 --> 00:27:11.160
Customers want to be able to run
their workloads on their choice

00:27:11.160 --> 00:27:13.060
of operating system.

00:27:13.060 --> 00:27:14.880
So these are some
of the main concerns

00:27:14.880 --> 00:27:16.950
as to customers have and
some of the motivation

00:27:16.950 --> 00:27:20.160
they have to actually reconsider
how these applications are

00:27:20.160 --> 00:27:22.020
architected.

00:27:22.020 --> 00:27:24.450
There are many ways to
modernize your applications.

00:27:24.450 --> 00:27:26.760
You could completely refactor.

00:27:26.760 --> 00:27:30.180
You could use more
managed services.

00:27:30.180 --> 00:27:32.659
Today we want to talk
about modernizing

00:27:32.659 --> 00:27:34.200
by moving to
containers, because this

00:27:34.200 --> 00:27:35.910
is one of the most
popular areas,

00:27:35.910 --> 00:27:37.790
and we've got a lot
of feedback over here

00:27:37.790 --> 00:27:40.140
that this is a really
great way that customers

00:27:40.140 --> 00:27:44.210
want to move their workloads
and modernize them.

00:27:44.210 --> 00:27:46.010
So let's see what
support we have for them.

00:27:46.010 --> 00:27:47.990
So today you can actually--

00:27:47.990 --> 00:27:50.690
Windows Server 2016 was the
first version of Windows that

00:27:50.690 --> 00:27:52.680
had support for containers.

00:27:52.680 --> 00:27:55.350
So it had support for
the Docker engine.

00:27:55.350 --> 00:27:59.150
We have support for container
optimized Windows Server

00:27:59.150 --> 00:27:59.690
images.

00:27:59.690 --> 00:28:02.420
So just like you have that
whole list of Windows images,

00:28:02.420 --> 00:28:06.290
you can actually take an
image, take a Windows container

00:28:06.290 --> 00:28:07.200
optimized image.

00:28:07.200 --> 00:28:11.390
It has your Docker images
already pulled for you

00:28:11.390 --> 00:28:13.130
with the right
networking settings

00:28:13.130 --> 00:28:15.950
and you can just deploy a
Windows container on it.

00:28:15.950 --> 00:28:17.750
If you want to go
more cross-platform,

00:28:17.750 --> 00:28:23.090
Microsoft introduced ASP.NET
Core or .NET Core technologies,

00:28:23.090 --> 00:28:26.120
and they've been constantly
investing in moving a lot

00:28:26.120 --> 00:28:28.640
of the libraries from
.NET to .NET Core.

00:28:28.640 --> 00:28:30.470
And this actually gives
you the flexibility

00:28:30.470 --> 00:28:32.720
of running it either
on a Windows container

00:28:32.720 --> 00:28:35.070
or on a Linux container.

00:28:35.070 --> 00:28:38.240
So if you were to kind of double
up on .NET Core and run it

00:28:38.240 --> 00:28:41.180
on a Linux container, you can
actually now move that entire

00:28:41.180 --> 00:28:45.380
workload running
on GC, GKE, or GAE.

00:28:45.380 --> 00:28:49.910
So GKE is Kubernetes,
managed Kubernetes service,

00:28:49.910 --> 00:28:51.320
and Google App Engine.

00:28:51.320 --> 00:28:54.560
So if you absolutely don't
want to do any management,

00:28:54.560 --> 00:28:57.260
GAE is your better bet.

00:28:59.950 --> 00:29:02.800
So a little bit about why you
should bring these containers

00:29:02.800 --> 00:29:04.332
to Google Cloud.

00:29:04.332 --> 00:29:06.040
Everything at Google
runs in a container.

00:29:06.040 --> 00:29:10.120
All the demos you see, all
the different services that we

00:29:10.120 --> 00:29:13.090
have, they all are
container based.

00:29:13.090 --> 00:29:16.510
We launch an average of about
four billion new containers

00:29:16.510 --> 00:29:17.590
per week.

00:29:17.590 --> 00:29:20.050
And this has really
allowed us to improve

00:29:20.050 --> 00:29:23.110
the tooling and our
systems for managing

00:29:23.110 --> 00:29:27.240
these containers at scale.

00:29:27.240 --> 00:29:31.890
We've also been contributing to
a pretty successful open source

00:29:31.890 --> 00:29:33.430
project, which I
think a lot of you

00:29:33.430 --> 00:29:35.340
will be familiar
with, Kubernetes.

00:29:35.340 --> 00:29:38.640
And we're constantly adding more
and more improvements to that

00:29:38.640 --> 00:29:41.850
as well as it's great to see
all the work that the developer

00:29:41.850 --> 00:29:45.334
community has done to make
this project successful.

00:29:45.334 --> 00:29:47.250
Just like we have support
for Linux containers

00:29:47.250 --> 00:29:50.170
and Kubernetes, there's
also an open source project

00:29:50.170 --> 00:29:52.830
and a working group that's
looking at Windows container

00:29:52.830 --> 00:29:57.580
support, and we are actively
involved in that group.

00:29:57.580 --> 00:30:01.950
So now let's kind of look at how
we can take our really, really

00:30:01.950 --> 00:30:05.460
old PetShop application
and modernize

00:30:05.460 --> 00:30:09.140
that using some of the
tools we just spoke about.

00:30:09.140 --> 00:30:11.946
So I'm handing it
over back to Simon.

00:30:11.946 --> 00:30:15.260
SIMON ZELTSER: Thanks, Amruta.

00:30:15.260 --> 00:30:18.440
So this is just a reminder
of the simple architecture

00:30:18.440 --> 00:30:20.450
of our PetShop application.

00:30:20.450 --> 00:30:25.280
And now what we want to do
is slightly modernize it.

00:30:25.280 --> 00:30:27.500
So in this case,
you can see that we

00:30:27.500 --> 00:30:31.490
will try to expose some
of that functionality

00:30:31.490 --> 00:30:33.260
as micro services.

00:30:33.260 --> 00:30:37.070
So we'll still have a front
end, which is Windows and IIS,

00:30:37.070 --> 00:30:40.250
and that will work against
three micro services, orders,

00:30:40.250 --> 00:30:42.480
product, and profile.

00:30:42.480 --> 00:30:46.220
Those in turn will work
against managed databases.

00:30:46.220 --> 00:30:48.320
So why doing that?

00:30:48.320 --> 00:30:54.320
So you get a lot of
architectural benefits

00:30:54.320 --> 00:30:58.070
from doing that such as
auto scaling or basically

00:30:58.070 --> 00:31:01.460
a lot of good
scenarios for doing

00:31:01.460 --> 00:31:04.940
scaling with Google
infrastructure

00:31:04.940 --> 00:31:10.760
as well as very good and easy
integration with Visual Studio

00:31:10.760 --> 00:31:15.870
and Stackdriver debugger, which
I'll show you in a few moments.

00:31:15.870 --> 00:31:17.550
So let's delve into the demo.

00:31:21.760 --> 00:31:24.530
So it's the same
PetShop application,

00:31:24.530 --> 00:31:30.430
but this time it will work using
a bit different architecture.

00:31:30.430 --> 00:31:37.630
So I already did some pre work
by exposing the functionality

00:31:37.630 --> 00:31:39.910
as micro services.

00:31:39.910 --> 00:31:43.280
If you have ASP.NET
MVC services and you

00:31:43.280 --> 00:31:46.060
want to move them
to ASP.NET Core,

00:31:46.060 --> 00:31:48.530
there is a relatively
easy path to do that.

00:31:48.530 --> 00:31:52.210
If not, you will have to
create your ASP.NET Core

00:31:52.210 --> 00:31:54.970
services from
scratch and gradually

00:31:54.970 --> 00:31:56.860
move your functionality over.

00:31:56.860 --> 00:31:58.100
So I already did that.

00:31:58.100 --> 00:32:02.410
And in this case, we'll talk
about taking that and basically

00:32:02.410 --> 00:32:04.750
doing containerization.

00:32:04.750 --> 00:32:06.240
So I added a Docker file.

00:32:06.240 --> 00:32:08.770
I'll demo that on one
of the micro services,

00:32:08.770 --> 00:32:11.390
but we need to do it
for the three of them.

00:32:11.390 --> 00:32:12.880
So this is the Docker file.

00:32:12.880 --> 00:32:16.660
You define the image, that base
image that you want to use,

00:32:16.660 --> 00:32:21.310
and in Google, we support
multiple ASP.NET Core images

00:32:21.310 --> 00:32:23.180
for different purposes.

00:32:23.180 --> 00:32:27.370
So you can base off of one of
the images that we support.

00:32:27.370 --> 00:32:30.550
You define how to run your
application, which parts

00:32:30.550 --> 00:32:32.920
to expose, and some of
the environment variables

00:32:32.920 --> 00:32:35.710
that may be useful for
running this properly

00:32:35.710 --> 00:32:39.310
on the Google Cloud.

00:32:39.310 --> 00:32:41.420
And then, essentially,
once we do that,

00:32:41.420 --> 00:32:44.080
we need to build
the container image

00:32:44.080 --> 00:32:47.920
and push it into a
container registry.

00:32:47.920 --> 00:32:54.190
So let's take a look at some
existing container registry

00:32:54.190 --> 00:32:55.960
images that are
already pre-created.

00:32:55.960 --> 00:32:58.790
So let's go into
Orders, for example.

00:32:58.790 --> 00:33:01.660
And you can see here that
these are different images

00:33:01.660 --> 00:33:02.590
that they pushed.

00:33:02.590 --> 00:33:03.910
I can tag them.

00:33:03.910 --> 00:33:05.590
And then essentially,
the whole point

00:33:05.590 --> 00:33:09.280
is that we can create
Kubernetes cluster, which

00:33:09.280 --> 00:33:12.520
will run our micro
services in containers

00:33:12.520 --> 00:33:16.630
using one of the images that we
pushed into container registry.

00:33:16.630 --> 00:33:21.290
And then we can get a lot
of benefits of Kubernetes.

00:33:21.290 --> 00:33:25.290
So let's take a look
at some of that.

00:33:25.290 --> 00:33:30.140
So what I can do is run
a command line cloud

00:33:30.140 --> 00:33:30.890
as the case shell.

00:33:33.920 --> 00:33:36.730
So I'll run a command which
will show that we already

00:33:36.730 --> 00:33:41.260
have a cluster, Kubernetes
cluster, that I pre-created.

00:33:41.260 --> 00:33:43.730
I'll show you in a
moment how to do that.

00:33:43.730 --> 00:33:46.450
So this command can
show you that we

00:33:46.450 --> 00:33:49.600
run the micro services in pods.

00:33:49.600 --> 00:33:53.410
And pod is the minimal
deployment unit for Kubernetes.

00:33:53.410 --> 00:33:55.960
So you scale your
micro services in pods.

00:33:55.960 --> 00:33:58.240
You deploy them into pods.

00:33:58.240 --> 00:34:00.220
And you can see that
for every micro service,

00:34:00.220 --> 00:34:02.680
we have one pod up and running.

00:34:02.680 --> 00:34:05.140
In order to create
Kubernetes cluster,

00:34:05.140 --> 00:34:09.010
you can just go
into this web UI.

00:34:09.010 --> 00:34:11.949
Under Kubernetes Engine,
there is an option

00:34:11.949 --> 00:34:15.130
to do that by clicking
on Create Cluster.

00:34:15.130 --> 00:34:19.480
There are also other options
available, such as command line

00:34:19.480 --> 00:34:21.380
tools that you can use.

00:34:21.380 --> 00:34:23.350
So essentially, to
create the cluster,

00:34:23.350 --> 00:34:26.620
you just need to
fill up the name

00:34:26.620 --> 00:34:29.980
of the cluster, the location,
the version of Kubernetes

00:34:29.980 --> 00:34:32.920
that you want to
deploy, and then there

00:34:32.920 --> 00:34:35.870
are certain features that
you can make available,

00:34:35.870 --> 00:34:38.739
such as Stackdriver
logging and monitoring.

00:34:38.739 --> 00:34:42.070
And if you open this
More thing and then

00:34:42.070 --> 00:34:44.679
scroll all the way
down, you can see

00:34:44.679 --> 00:34:47.889
that we are pretty
secure offering in here,

00:34:47.889 --> 00:34:51.429
because we can
basically define what

00:34:51.429 --> 00:34:54.850
Google services will be
available from that Kubernetes

00:34:54.850 --> 00:34:55.750
cluster.

00:34:55.750 --> 00:34:57.610
I can do that for all the API.

00:34:57.610 --> 00:35:00.310
I can do that with
full granularity.

00:35:00.310 --> 00:35:02.590
So in our later
sample, we're going

00:35:02.590 --> 00:35:06.880
to show how we can
use Cloud Debugger.

00:35:06.880 --> 00:35:10.989
So for that, we need to go all
the way down and then enable

00:35:10.989 --> 00:35:13.030
Cloud Debugging feature
and then, essentially, we

00:35:13.030 --> 00:35:16.390
can click on Create and that
will create our Kubernetes

00:35:16.390 --> 00:35:17.540
cluster.

00:35:17.540 --> 00:35:19.540
So let's say that we have
Kubernetes cluster up

00:35:19.540 --> 00:35:21.250
and running already.

00:35:21.250 --> 00:35:23.600
Let's see what we
can do with this.

00:35:23.600 --> 00:35:27.880
So one thing I can do is just
run kubectl get deployments.

00:35:30.560 --> 00:35:32.700
Now, kubectl is the
command line tool

00:35:32.700 --> 00:35:34.430
to manage your
Kubernetes clusters.

00:35:34.430 --> 00:35:36.710
For those who are
not familiar, we

00:35:36.710 --> 00:35:40.670
are going to use this to
talk to our PetShop cluster

00:35:40.670 --> 00:35:42.350
that I already pre-created.

00:35:42.350 --> 00:35:45.290
You can see that we have
three deployments here.

00:35:45.290 --> 00:35:49.460
One and one meaning that
we wanted one copy of each

00:35:49.460 --> 00:35:51.980
and we actually get
one copy of each.

00:35:51.980 --> 00:35:55.640
But the whole power is what
happens when we want to scale.

00:35:55.640 --> 00:35:58.820
So with Kubernetes, you get
this scaling capabilities

00:35:58.820 --> 00:36:01.010
basically for free,
and it makes it

00:36:01.010 --> 00:36:05.300
very efficient by
taking care of all

00:36:05.300 --> 00:36:07.400
the scalability work for you.

00:36:07.400 --> 00:36:13.730
So you can do kubectl
scale deployment.

00:36:13.730 --> 00:36:16.820
And let's say we want
to scale order service.

00:36:19.430 --> 00:36:21.070
And I want to have
three replicas.

00:36:24.120 --> 00:36:27.480
So what this command does,
it basically tells Kubernetes

00:36:27.480 --> 00:36:31.680
that we want to spin up two more
instances of our deployment,

00:36:31.680 --> 00:36:34.350
and Kubernetes will do all
the heavy lifting for us.

00:36:34.350 --> 00:36:38.730
And to kind of check on
that, we can just run,

00:36:38.730 --> 00:36:41.820
again, the kubectl
get deployments.

00:36:41.820 --> 00:36:45.480
And you can see here that now
the desired and the current

00:36:45.480 --> 00:36:49.050
changed to the three,
just like we asked.

00:36:49.050 --> 00:36:56.190
We can also run kubectl get
pods and see these are the three

00:36:56.190 --> 00:36:58.840
instances that were created.

00:36:58.840 --> 00:37:01.620
So we basically just
run one single command

00:37:01.620 --> 00:37:05.040
and Kubernetes was able
to scale our ASP.NET Core

00:37:05.040 --> 00:37:08.560
application really easy.

00:37:08.560 --> 00:37:09.500
Now let's scale back.

00:37:13.780 --> 00:37:17.100
So in this case, I'll just
go back to one replica.

00:37:17.100 --> 00:37:20.110
And we can run get pods again.

00:37:20.110 --> 00:37:23.760
And in this case, you can
see that the two replicas

00:37:23.760 --> 00:37:27.420
that we want to kill there are
in the process of being killed.

00:37:27.420 --> 00:37:32.460
And essentially, we'll have one
instance of every micro service

00:37:32.460 --> 00:37:34.530
up and running.

00:37:34.530 --> 00:37:35.940
OK, so that's all good.

00:37:35.940 --> 00:37:40.020
We can scale with Kubernetes
and Kubernetes will take care

00:37:40.020 --> 00:37:42.030
of the scaling capabilities.

00:37:42.030 --> 00:37:44.070
But there is more to that.

00:37:44.070 --> 00:37:49.060
So what if we want to handle
resiliency for our services?

00:37:49.060 --> 00:37:54.990
So let's try to kill one of the
pods, which will essentially

00:37:54.990 --> 00:37:58.000
kill our micro service,
and see what happens.

00:37:58.000 --> 00:38:00.810
So first of all, I'll run
kubectl get pods again

00:38:00.810 --> 00:38:04.780
to just kind of confirm
the starting state.

00:38:04.780 --> 00:38:08.340
And now we basically want to
kill one of the micro services

00:38:08.340 --> 00:38:12.440
and see what happens once it
runs under Kubernetes cluster.

00:38:12.440 --> 00:38:16.260
So kubectl delete pod.

00:38:16.260 --> 00:38:20.310
And I'll just copy
the name of that pod.

00:38:20.310 --> 00:38:22.390
And you can see
that it was deleted.

00:38:22.390 --> 00:38:25.500
Now if I run kubectl
get pods, you

00:38:25.500 --> 00:38:28.540
can see two copies
of that profile.

00:38:28.540 --> 00:38:30.660
Sorry, so one is
terminating, and another

00:38:30.660 --> 00:38:33.090
is running, which is
actually very interesting.

00:38:33.090 --> 00:38:36.930
Because what happens
here is that the one

00:38:36.930 --> 00:38:40.830
that is terminating is that
copy that we tried to kill.

00:38:40.830 --> 00:38:44.640
So it takes time until
it actually gets killed.

00:38:44.640 --> 00:38:46.590
But then Kubernetes
was smart enough

00:38:46.590 --> 00:38:51.060
to recognize that we desire to
run one instance of a profile

00:38:51.060 --> 00:38:51.870
service.

00:38:51.870 --> 00:38:56.290
So if your service was affected
and essentially failed,

00:38:56.290 --> 00:38:59.430
Kubernetes will bring
another copy of that service

00:38:59.430 --> 00:39:01.410
back up automatically for you.

00:39:01.410 --> 00:39:03.570
If you have multiple
copies, it will do that

00:39:03.570 --> 00:39:05.530
with multiple copies as well.

00:39:05.530 --> 00:39:08.970
So by just moving to Kubernetes
with your ASP.NET Core

00:39:08.970 --> 00:39:11.970
workload, what
you're able to get

00:39:11.970 --> 00:39:16.260
is full handling of
resiliency as well as scaling

00:39:16.260 --> 00:39:20.760
capabilities, which is
really, really cool.

00:39:20.760 --> 00:39:23.610
Now, another thing that
we might want to consider

00:39:23.610 --> 00:39:27.990
is the feature called
Stackdriver Debugger.

00:39:27.990 --> 00:39:30.600
Now, this feature
currently is in preview.

00:39:30.600 --> 00:39:32.760
But the fact that we are
running in Google Cloud

00:39:32.760 --> 00:39:37.140
Platform with ASP.NET Core
allows us to do the following.

00:39:37.140 --> 00:39:42.570
So I'll open my debug screen
on the Google Cloud Console.

00:39:42.570 --> 00:39:45.870
And you will see here
that this is basically

00:39:45.870 --> 00:39:48.180
the screen where
we can production

00:39:48.180 --> 00:39:50.230
debug our application.

00:39:50.230 --> 00:39:53.710
Now, it sounds like
a bold statement.

00:39:53.710 --> 00:39:57.000
But this is actually what it is.

00:39:57.000 --> 00:39:59.280
What this application
allows us to do

00:39:59.280 --> 00:40:02.910
is opening the source
code of our application,

00:40:02.910 --> 00:40:06.990
attaching the break point to
existing running application

00:40:06.990 --> 00:40:10.140
in production, and then
inspecting the snapshot

00:40:10.140 --> 00:40:12.970
that Stackdriver will
take from our application

00:40:12.970 --> 00:40:15.600
if we hit that particular
code pass, which

00:40:15.600 --> 00:40:17.040
leads to the break point.

00:40:17.040 --> 00:40:20.320
So let's try and see
how we can do that.

00:40:20.320 --> 00:40:23.040
So in this case, I can
bring my source code

00:40:23.040 --> 00:40:24.690
from multiple locations.

00:40:24.690 --> 00:40:29.040
We have integration with GitHub,
GitLab, Bitbucket, Cloud Source

00:40:29.040 --> 00:40:32.460
Repositories of Google Cloud,
as well as you can also

00:40:32.460 --> 00:40:34.560
bring your own sources.

00:40:34.560 --> 00:40:35.700
So this is what I will do.

00:40:35.700 --> 00:40:42.520
I'll take my local sources
and I'll hit Upload.

00:40:42.520 --> 00:40:45.270
Now, this is not really
uploading into Cloud.

00:40:45.270 --> 00:40:49.150
This is just opening that
locally on my browser.

00:40:49.150 --> 00:40:52.190
And you can see this is the
code of the application.

00:40:52.190 --> 00:40:56.210
We'll try to debug
service code products.

00:40:56.210 --> 00:40:58.660
And if we explore
some of its code,

00:40:58.660 --> 00:41:02.410
there is this
category controller.

00:41:02.410 --> 00:41:07.530
And we can just go and
explore what it does.

00:41:07.530 --> 00:41:11.530
But one of the actions
here called category.

00:41:11.530 --> 00:41:14.930
And we can see that it
tries to use caching.

00:41:14.930 --> 00:41:18.000
So if caching is
enabled, we might use it.

00:41:18.000 --> 00:41:19.890
Otherwise we will not use it.

00:41:19.890 --> 00:41:23.460
So let's inspect whether
caching is actually enabled.

00:41:23.460 --> 00:41:27.080
So that this kind of modeling
the real world scenario

00:41:27.080 --> 00:41:29.600
where you have something
running in production.

00:41:29.600 --> 00:41:31.040
Something happens.

00:41:31.040 --> 00:41:33.680
You want to understand what is
the state of your application

00:41:33.680 --> 00:41:37.070
without re-deploying with
additional instrumentation

00:41:37.070 --> 00:41:38.820
that you might want to add.

00:41:38.820 --> 00:41:41.840
So you can just add your
break point at the place

00:41:41.840 --> 00:41:43.220
where you want to stop.

00:41:43.220 --> 00:41:47.990
And now it's waiting
to hit that code pass.

00:41:47.990 --> 00:41:52.770
Now, how do we know how
to get into this method?

00:41:52.770 --> 00:41:55.880
So in Kubernetes,
I did some pre-work

00:41:55.880 --> 00:41:59.460
which was exposing all my micro
services with external IP.

00:41:59.460 --> 00:42:02.360
So I created load balancer
object and I exposed

00:42:02.360 --> 00:42:05.870
my micro services externally.

00:42:05.870 --> 00:42:09.740
So I'll run kubectl
get services.

00:42:09.740 --> 00:42:12.260
And this will return all
of my services running

00:42:12.260 --> 00:42:15.500
under Kubernetes with
their external IP.

00:42:15.500 --> 00:42:17.570
We are talking about
the product service.

00:42:17.570 --> 00:42:20.970
So I'll just copy this
external IP from here.

00:42:24.580 --> 00:42:26.910
And if we look at
the source code,

00:42:26.910 --> 00:42:29.350
there is this route
called category.

00:42:29.350 --> 00:42:32.430
So in order to reach this
code pass, what we need to do

00:42:32.430 --> 00:42:37.750
is go into this IP and
then slash category.

00:42:37.750 --> 00:42:39.000
So let's do that.

00:42:39.000 --> 00:42:42.060
So this opens some
information, which

00:42:42.060 --> 00:42:45.790
is categories of our PetShop.

00:42:45.790 --> 00:42:48.460
And let's go back into debugger.

00:42:48.460 --> 00:42:51.850
So you can see here that
suddenly it shows all the call

00:42:51.850 --> 00:42:54.420
stack of that code pass.

00:42:54.420 --> 00:42:56.770
So basically, we
hit the break point.

00:42:56.770 --> 00:43:00.250
There is the call stack
of what really happens up

00:43:00.250 --> 00:43:02.890
until that point.

00:43:02.890 --> 00:43:04.880
This is the real application.

00:43:04.880 --> 00:43:10.890
And also we are able to
see here all the variables

00:43:10.890 --> 00:43:14.890
and their state at that moment
when this snapshot was taken.

00:43:14.890 --> 00:43:22.040
So I can open up this
state and then take

00:43:22.040 --> 00:43:24.320
a look at this caching
enabled variable.

00:43:24.320 --> 00:43:26.960
You can see it's 0,
which means false.

00:43:26.960 --> 00:43:29.540
This kind of gives
me the idea of what's

00:43:29.540 --> 00:43:31.770
going on with my application.

00:43:31.770 --> 00:43:37.050
Now, all those snapshots are
really a powerful feature.

00:43:37.050 --> 00:43:39.860
And there is almost no
overhead to run that.

00:43:39.860 --> 00:43:41.960
So you can run that
in production safely.

00:43:41.960 --> 00:43:44.660
You can be sure
that it will not run

00:43:44.660 --> 00:43:47.090
on all of your CPU or memory.

00:43:47.090 --> 00:43:50.540
The overhead is almost
next to nothing.

00:43:50.540 --> 00:43:52.760
And it gives you this
powerful capability

00:43:52.760 --> 00:43:58.430
of exploring your application
state if you really need that.

00:43:58.430 --> 00:44:01.330
And then there is also
this snapshot history.

00:44:01.330 --> 00:44:04.600
So you can share this
state with other developers

00:44:04.600 --> 00:44:08.170
by just sending them link
into the relevant snapshot

00:44:08.170 --> 00:44:13.210
and essentially
get to know what's

00:44:13.210 --> 00:44:16.880
going on with your application.

00:44:16.880 --> 00:44:18.220
All right, so let's switch back.

00:44:22.170 --> 00:44:24.490
AMRUTA GULANIKAR:
Cool, so hopefully we

00:44:24.490 --> 00:44:27.490
demonstrated to you how easy
it was to just lift and shift.

00:44:27.490 --> 00:44:29.930
And Simon just
demonstrated to you

00:44:29.930 --> 00:44:32.530
some of the amazing
capabilities that just light up

00:44:32.530 --> 00:44:35.290
if you were to re-factor and
move this to Microsoft Services

00:44:35.290 --> 00:44:37.360
on GKE.

00:44:37.360 --> 00:44:40.900
A lot of the work was
sort of done and sort of

00:44:40.900 --> 00:44:43.990
explained quickly because
we have a short time,

00:44:43.990 --> 00:44:46.670
but we have a detailed
set of White Papers

00:44:46.670 --> 00:44:50.920
that we wrote with one of our
Windows partners, Magenic,

00:44:50.920 --> 00:44:53.650
and they go into an extremely
detailed explanation

00:44:53.650 --> 00:44:56.320
for the same application
of how you could move

00:44:56.320 --> 00:44:58.990
and the different
pieces and the tools.

00:44:58.990 --> 00:45:02.074
And all the work done
is actually available.

00:45:02.074 --> 00:45:04.240
The code's available, so
you're free to kind of look

00:45:04.240 --> 00:45:07.750
at some of the examples
and see how we did

00:45:07.750 --> 00:45:09.740
some of the migration there.

00:45:09.740 --> 00:45:11.740
In addition to that, we'd
like to also leave you

00:45:11.740 --> 00:45:12.970
with some of the links.

00:45:12.970 --> 00:45:16.940
So if you go to
cloud.google.com/windows or SQL

00:45:16.940 --> 00:45:19.840
Server, we have a lot more
information and White Papers

00:45:19.840 --> 00:45:21.730
and best practices.

00:45:21.730 --> 00:45:24.861
And we also have, similarly,
information about our developer

00:45:24.861 --> 00:45:25.360
tools.

00:45:25.360 --> 00:45:28.370
So we will probably be
